    verification stpaDiagramInTheBinaryReviewGate : Test { :>> id = "df669da2-bde3-45ff-acdb-bfd681c6ec8c"; :>> title = "Sprint 659 review gate — three passes per D0172"; :>> createdAt = "2026-09-10"; :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus5"; :>> method = VerificationMethod::inspect; :>> procedureText = "Three passes (D0172). Substance: the diagram is now a computed view of a computed view - the same binary that computes the structure draws it, so the picture cannot lag the structure, and the five properties the skill used to ask a reader to check by eye are asserted on every cargo test. The byte-equality discipline paid for itself twice: it is what found the lens's nondeterminism (a computed view varying with nothing in the tree changed, which every consumer of the JSON had been living with), and it is what made the port's correctness a fact rather than a resemblance. Record: three Issues, all found by running the thing rather than reading it; the nondeterminism is in-field (observed on the binary, three runs), the console defect is code-read then confirmed at runtime, and the empty touched set is in-field and the most consequential - the D0425 flow's verifier had, for its second sprint, run a set that was empty whenever the previous sprint had landed, and only the verifier's habit of reporting the receipt's own counts rather than the claim exposed it. Stated plainly: the CLI's stdout differs from the Python tool's by one trailing newline that cmd_view0 adds to every lens; the DoD's byte-for-byte clause is met by the render function and the fixture test, and the stdout byte is named in the DoD receipt rather than special-cased away. Not delivered by design: no stored SVG (a view), no change to what the structure computes, no brief republish (D0407 still proposed)."; }
